From 01c47ca35839ab291344e8c49cdac0e752f95197 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeff Queisser <funkzy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 12:42:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 41/58] wifi: apple: centauri: Connect to the BSS the request
 names

A connect request carries the BSSID and channel the client chose from
its own scan results. The driver looked the target up in cfg80211's
scan list instead and refused the connect with -ENOENT when the entry
was not there. iwd runs a requested scan as channel subsets, each with
the flush flag, and at the end of each subset cfg80211 expires every
BSS that subset did not see, on every band. After a last subset of
2.4 GHz channels with no access point, the list holds none of the
network's BSSes while iwd's own list holds all of them, so every connect
right after such a scan failed until a later unflushed scan refilled
cfg80211's list.

- The target is the request's BSSID and channel, or its hints. The
  scan entry is looked up only when the request names neither.
- The RSN capabilities sent to the firmware are the station's own, from
  the request's RSN element (the client's MFP choice), rather than the
  AP's from the scan entry.
- A successful connect is reported against cfg80211's entry for the
  target. When the client's scans expired it, one is informed from the
  request (BSSID, channel, SSID), and the AP's beacons, which the
  firmware reports once associated, fill in its elements.
- Scan and connect failures name the firmware's status: a refused
  command, a connect or disconnect event with a non-zero status, a scan
  whose completion event carries one, or no event at all. command()
  still returns -EREMOTEIO for a refusal.

Signed-off-by: Jeff Queisser <funkzy@users.noreply.github.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>

(cherry picked from commit af24bd0442053dd6a9de72c0af2a625600fb1269)
[n1-wifi: on aurora-j716s this driver replaces integration's J813/J873 N1 driver files]
